data: {"invocationId":"e-9c119957-7ec4-4308-9e8b-192705178b51","author":"corkboard","actions":{"stateDelta":{"round1":{"industry_expert":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Fix missed calls with AI, but verify HIPAA and Dentrix integration first.","summary":"As an experienced operator, I see dental practices lose good new patients simply because the single front desk is busy. An AI receptionist stops missed calls within your budget. However, you must confirm software integration and compliance before signing.","key_points":["Dental practices miss up to 30 percent of calls, and callers rarely leave voicemails (F5 unverified).","Specialized dental AI tools cost between $150 and $500 per month, fitting your budget (F3 unverified).","Deep integration with Dentrix lets the AI book appointments directly into your calendar (F4 unverified)."],"risks":["AI might fail to handle complex clinical triage from existing patients.","Vendor may not sign a Business Associate Agreement for HIPAA compliance (F1 unverified).","Maryland call recording laws require proper consent disclosures for automated recordings (F2 unverified)."],"confidence":80,"pushback":"Ask your attorney or the local Small Business Development Center to confirm if the AI vendor provides a valid BAA for HIPAA compliance regarding voice data."},"ai_tools_expert":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Yes to AI, but only with proper integrations and legal checks.","summary":"Dental practices miss up to 30 percent of incoming calls, losing patients to competitors (F5). An AI receptionist can capture these leads within your $400 budget (F3) and connect to RingCentral (F6). However, you must verify compliance and Dentrix integration before signing.","key_points":["Dental practices miss nearly a third of calls, and callers rarely leave voicemails (F5).","Specialized dental AI tools cost between $150 and $500 monthly, fitting your budget (F3).","Tools can connect directly to RingCentral without a carrier migration (F6)."],"risks":["Failing to secure a signed Business Associate Agreement (BAA) violates HIPAA (F1).","Maryland call-recording laws require strict two-party consent setup (F2).","An AI might fail to handle complex clinical triage for existing patients."],"confidence":80,"pushback":"Assume the AI will seamlessly book in Dentrix without testing real-time calendar syncing first.","tools":[{"name":"Smith.ai","use":"Provides AI-driven voice receptionists with dental scheduling integrations.","monthly_cost":"$150 to $300/mo","works_with":"Connects via RingCentral and calendar sync","caveat":"Must confirm they sign a BAA for HIPAA compliance (unverified, confirm with attorney)."}]},"realist":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Block signing until legal compliance and vendor BAA are confirmed.","summary":"I am putting a conditional hold on signing this month. While specialized dental AI tools fit your $400 budget, we face strict HIPAA and Maryland two-party recording laws that block moving forward blindly. We must verify vendor contracts and state compliance first.","key_points":["Specialized dental AI tools cost between $150 and $500 per month, fitting your budget (F3).","Maryland is a two-party consent state, impacting how AI handles call recording disclosures (F2).","AI must integrate with Dentrix to book appointments directly instead of just taking messages (F4)."],"risks":["Failing to secure a signed BAA violates HIPAA rules.","Breaking Maryland call-recording laws exposes the practice to legal penalties.","An improper plan size could exceed the $400 monthly budget."],"confidence":90,"pushback":"Do you assume any off-the-shelf AI tool is automatically HIPAA-compliant and legally safe for dental use?","checks":[{"area":"compliance","status":"block","note":"HIPAA requires a signed Business Associate Agreement (BAA) from the AI vendor (F1, unverified; confirm with attorney)."},{"area":"legal","status":"block","note":"Maryland is a two-party consent state for call recording, requiring proper automated disclosures (F2, unverified; confirm with attorney)."},{"area":"financial","status":"pass","note":"Pricing for specialized dental AI tools falls within the $150 to $500 range, aligning with your $400 budget (F3)."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"An AI tool directly addresses missing new-patient calls and front-desk overload (F5)."}]},"strategist":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Sign up only if the vendor signs a BAA first.","summary":"Fixing missed calls protects revenue. But locking into AI commits you to complex workflows, compliance checks, and patient trust hurdles. We must trace the fallout before writing a check.","key_points":["Next week, setup commits us to verifying HIPAA compliance via a BAA (F1) and complying with Maryland two-party consent rules for call recording (F2).","Next quarter, deep Dentrix integration unlocks direct booking instead of simple message-taking, reducing front-desk friction (F4).","Next year, this locks us into an automated workflow that reduces missed calls which currently sit at up to 30 percent industry-wide (F5)."],"risks":["Failing to secure a signed BAA exposes the practice to massive HIPAA penalties.","Patients needing complex clinical triage may get frustrated by an AI bot.","Switching tools later incurs high migration costs and retraining burdens."],"confidence":75,"pushback":"What move follows this? You must audit call volumes and consult an attorney regarding Maryland consent laws before launch."},"devils_advocate":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Do not rush into an AI receptionist before checking legal risks.","summary":"Signing up for an AI receptionist this month is too risky. Maryland is a two-party consent state for call recording, which affects automated recordings (F2). We also lack a confirmed Business Associate Agreement for HIPAA compliance (F1). We must verify these legal requirements with an attorney or accountant before buying.","key_points":["Maryland requires all parties to consent to call recording, which complicates automated AI greetings (F2).","Handling patient names and appointments via AI requires a signed Business Associate Agreement for HIPAA compliance (F1).","Specialized dental AI tools can cost up to $500 per month, which may exceed our $400 budget depending on call volume (F3)."],"risks":["Failing to secure a BAA from the AI vendor could trigger severe HIPAA violations.","Automated call recording without proper consent disclosures could violate Maryland state law.","An AI receptionist might mishandle complex clinical triage that requires human judgment."],"confidence":85,"pushback":"Assume that rushing to buy an AI tool this month will solve missed calls without creating new legal and compliance liabilities."}}},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":"ROUND 2 — rebuttal. Below is every seat's Round 1 position. Respond to other seats by name. Change your stance only if a specific argument persuades you, and name it in changed_because. Do not drift toward agreement for its own sake. Return your full updated opinion.\n\n## Round 1\n\n### Industry Expert (industry_expert) — conditional, confidence 80\nHeadline: Fix missed calls with AI, but verify HIPAA and Dentrix integration first.\nSummary: As an experienced operator, I see dental practices lose good new patients simply because the single front desk is busy. An AI receptionist stops missed calls within your budget. However, you must confirm software integration and compliance before signing.\nPoints: Dental practices miss up to 30 percent of calls, and callers rarely leave voicemails (F5 unverified). | Specialized dental AI tools cost between $150 and $500 per month, fitting your budget (F3 unverified). | Deep integration with Dentrix lets the AI book appointments directly into your calendar (F4 unverified).\nRisks: AI might fail to handle complex clinical triage from existing patients. | Vendor may not sign a Business Associate Agreement for HIPAA compliance (F1 unverified). | Maryland call recording laws require proper consent disclosures for automated recordings (F2 unverified).\nPushback: Ask your attorney or the local Small Business Development Center to confirm if the AI vendor provides a valid BAA for HIPAA compliance regarding voice data.\n\n### AI-Tools Expert (ai_tools_expert) — conditional, confidence 80\nHeadline: Yes to AI, but only with proper integrations and legal checks.\nSummary: Dental practices miss up to 30 percent of incoming calls, losing patients to competitors (F5). An AI receptionist can capture these leads within your $400 budget (F3) and connect to RingCentral (F6). However, you must verify compliance and Dentrix integration before signing.\nPoints: Dental practices miss nearly a third of calls, and callers rarely leave voicemails (F5). | Specialized dental AI tools cost between $150 and $500 monthly, fitting your budget (F3). | Tools can connect directly to RingCentral without a carrier migration (F6).\nRisks: Failing to secure a signed Business Associate Agreement (BAA) violates HIPAA (F1). | Maryland call-recording laws require strict two-party consent setup (F2). | An AI might fail to handle complex clinical triage for existing patients.\nPushback: Assume the AI will seamlessly book in Dentrix without testing real-time calendar syncing first.\nTool: Smith.ai ($150 to $300/mo) — Provides AI-driven voice receptionists with dental scheduling integrations. Caveat: Must confirm they sign a BAA for HIPAA compliance (unverified, confirm with attorney).\n\n### Realist (realist) — conditional, confidence 90\nHeadline: Block signing until legal compliance and vendor BAA are confirmed.\nSummary: I am putting a conditional hold on signing this month. While specialized dental AI tools fit your $400 budget, we face strict HIPAA and Maryland two-party recording laws that block moving forward blindly. We must verify vendor contracts and state compliance first.\nPoints: Specialized dental AI tools cost between $150 and $500 per month, fitting your budget (F3). | Maryland is a two-party consent state, impacting how AI handles call recording disclosures (F2). | AI must integrate with Dentrix to book appointments directly instead of just taking messages (F4).\nRisks: Failing to secure a signed BAA violates HIPAA rules. | Breaking Maryland call-recording laws exposes the practice to legal penalties. | An improper plan size could exceed the $400 monthly budget.\nPushback: Do you assume any off-the-shelf AI tool is automatically HIPAA-compliant and legally safe for dental use?\nCheck [compliance: block] HIPAA requires a signed Business Associate Agreement (BAA) from the AI vendor (F1, unverified; confirm with attorney).\nCheck [legal: block] Maryland is a two-party consent state for call recording, requiring proper automated disclosures (F2, unverified; confirm with attorney).\nCheck [financial: pass] Pricing for specialized dental AI tools falls within the $150 to $500 range, aligning with your $400 budget (F3).\nCheck [goals: pass] An AI tool directly addresses missing new-patient calls and front-desk overload (F5).\n\n### Strategist (strategist) — conditional, confidence 75\nHeadline: Sign up only if the vendor signs a BAA first.\nSummary: Fixing missed calls protects revenue. But locking into AI commits you to complex workflows, compliance checks, and patient trust hurdles. We must trace the fallout before writing a check.\nPoints: Next week, setup commits us to verifying HIPAA compliance via a BAA (F1) and complying with Maryland two-party consent rules for call recording (F2). | Next quarter, deep Dentrix integration unlocks direct booking instead of simple message-taking, reducing front-desk friction (F4). | Next year, this locks us into an automated workflow that reduces missed calls which currently sit at up to 30 percent industry-wide (F5).\nRisks: Failing to secure a signed BAA exposes the practice to massive HIPAA penalties. | Patients needing complex clinical triage may get frustrated by an AI bot. | Switching tools later incurs high migration costs and retraining burdens.\nPushback: What move follows this? You must audit call volumes and consult an attorney regarding Maryland consent laws before launch.\n\n### Devil's Advocate (devils_advocate) — oppose, confidence 85\nHeadline: Do not rush into an AI receptionist before checking legal risks.\nSummary: Signing up for an AI receptionist this month is too risky. Maryland is a two-party consent state for call recording, which affects automated recordings (F2). We also lack a confirmed Business Associate Agreement for HIPAA compliance (F1). We must verify these legal requirements with an attorney or accountant before buying.\nPoints: Maryland requires all parties to consent to call recording, which complicates automated AI greetings (F2). | Handling patient names and appointments via AI requires a signed Business Associate Agreement for HIPAA compliance (F1). | Specialized dental AI tools can cost up to $500 per month, which may exceed our $400 budget depending on call volume (F3).\nRisks: Failing to secure a BAA from the AI vendor could trigger severe HIPAA violations. | Automated call recording without proper consent disclosures could violate Maryland state law. | An AI receptionist might mishandle complex clinical triage that requires human judgment.\nPushback: Assume that rushing to buy an AI tool this month will solve missed calls without creating new legal and compliance liabilities.","nodeInfo":{"path":"corkboard@1/open_rebuttal@1","outputFor":["corkboard@1/open_rebuttal@1"]},"id":"aea43d9b-8f60-4fb3-8cfe-1815ac1de342","timestamp":1793993548.3715136}

